# Feature: DOR1 — a BIMOLECULAR rule whose rate is a single local function,
#          with only ONE of the two reactants tagged.
# Tests:   the four shapes a single tag can take on a two-reactant rule:
#            RB  tag on reactant pattern 1, molecule-level  (Sa + E()%x)
#            RA  tag on reactant pattern 0, molecule-level  (E()%x + Sb)
#            RC  tag on reactant pattern 1, pattern-level   (Sc + %x:E())
#            RD  pattern-level tag on a MULTI-molecule pattern
#          The tagged reactant contributes Σ_t w_t·f(t); the untagged one
#          contributes its plain embedding count, so the propensity is the
#          product of the two — NFsim's DOR1.
#
# Oracle:  deliberately unambiguous.  The enzyme context is a HETERODIMER
#          E(d!1).F(d!1), so `E()` matches each complex exactly once and
#          the per-molecule vs per-complex context-multiplicity question
#          (issue #33 — where NFsim and BNG2's network expansion genuinely
#          disagree by the number of matching molecules per complex) cannot
#          reach any arm here.  Verified: NFsim, BNG2 network → ODE, and RM
#          all agree on all four arms, so this model pins the DOR1
#          propensity itself and nothing else.  Reference is NFsim for
#          consistency with the rest of the tier; the ODE agrees with it
#          column for column.
#
#          `Mod` counts a site on BOTH molecules of the complex, so the two
#          observable scopes still differ by a clean 2x without leaning on
#          any contested counting rule.
#
# Tier:    network-free.
# Expected (kc = 1e-3, 20 heterodimers, Mod = 40 throughout — no rule
#          touches E or F):
#            RA/RB  molecule-level: Mod(x) = 1 on the tagged E
#                   -> c = 20·kc = 0.02 per substrate
#            RC/RD  complex-wide:   Mod(x) = 2 across E and F
#                   -> c = 40·kc = 0.04 per substrate
#          BNG2's own network confirms the same split, emitting
#          `_RB_local1 = kc*1` / `_RC_local1 = kc*2`.  Each substrate is then
#          an exact binomial death process, Sub(t) ~ Binomial(300, e^-ct).
# Regression: before issue #34 no propensity branch covered `is_local` with
#          molecularity 2.  The rule fired once, then read a never-populated
#          accumulator as its propensity and went inert — silently, with the
#          substrate observable simply flat.
# invariant: conserved Mod = 40

begin model
begin parameters
    kc    1.0e-3
    S0    300
end parameters

begin molecule types
    Sa(s~0~1)
    Sb(s~0~1)
    Sc(s~0~1)
    Sd(s~0~1)
    E(d,e~0~1)
    F(d,f~0~1)
end molecule types

begin seed species
    Sa(s~0)                 S0
    Sb(s~0)                 S0
    Sc(s~0)                 S0
    Sd(s~0)                 S0
    E(d!1,e~1).F(d!1,f~1)   20
end seed species

begin observables
    Molecules  SubA   Sa(s~0)
    Molecules  SubB   Sb(s~0)
    Molecules  SubC   Sc(s~0)
    Molecules  SubD   Sd(s~0)
    # One site on each partner, so molecule scope sees 1 and complex-wide
    # scope sees 2 — with only one embedding per complex either way.
    Molecules  Mod    E(e~1), F(f~1)
end observables

begin functions
    # Molecule-level tag -> Mod(x) counts within the tagged E itself  (= 1).
    # Pattern-level tag  -> Mod(x) counts across x's whole complex     (= 2).
    lf(x) = kc*Mod(x)
end functions

begin reaction rules
    # Tagged reactant SECOND (the shape reported in issue #34).
    RB: Sa(s~0) + E()%x  ->  Sa(s~1) + E()%x   lf(x)
    # Tagged reactant FIRST — the factor has to follow the tag to the other
    # sampler slot, not sit on whichever reactant happens to be pattern 0.
    RA: E()%x + Sb(s~0)  ->  E()%x + Sb(s~1)   lf(x)
    # Pattern-level tag: complex-wide observable scope, single-molecule
    # pattern.  A per-molecule misreading of the scope halves this arm.
    RC: Sc(s~0) + %x:E()  ->  Sc(s~1) + %x:E()   lf(x)
    # Pattern-level tag on a two-molecule reactant pattern: the untagged side
    # is a plain count, the tagged side a multi-molecule embedding count.
    RD: Sd(s~0) + %x:E(d!1).F(d!1)  ->  Sd(s~1) + %x:E(d!1).F(d!1)   lf(x)
end reaction rules
end model

begin actions
simulate({method=>"nf",t_end=>60,n_steps=>60})
end actions
